---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Dispatch from '../../components/Dispatch.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site.js';
---

<Base
  title="How Long Should a Pool Pump Run in Texas?"
  description="The one-turnover rule for pool pump run time, adjusted for Central Texas summers and variable-speed schedules. Call (512) 764-9867."
>
  <h1>How long should a pool pump run per day in Central Texas?</h1>

  <p>
    The standard rule of thumb is running the pump long enough to turn over (filter) the pool's
    entire water volume roughly once a day, but in a Central Texas summer, that often means 8 to
    12 hours rather than the 6 hours sometimes quoted nationally — higher heat accelerates algae
    growth and chlorine burn-off enough that a single turnover a day, done quickly, may not be
    keeping up.
  </p>

  <Dispatch problem="Not sure the pump is running long enough" area={SITE.serviceArea} />

  <h2>Where the "one turnover a day" rule comes from</h2>
  <p>
    A turnover is the time it takes your pump and filter to cycle the pool's total water volume
    through the system once. It's a reasonable baseline for water clarity in mild conditions, but
    it was never a Texas-specific number — it's a national average that doesn't account for how
    much faster heat drives chemical demand and biological growth here.
  </p>

  <h2>Adjusting the number for Texas seasons</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <strong>Summer.</strong> 8–12 hours a day is typical for single-speed pumps in peak heat.
      Pools with heavy bather load or a lot of tree debris may need the higher end of that range.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Spring and fall.</strong> 6–8 hours is usually enough as temperatures moderate,
      though spring oak pollen (see our{' '}
      <a href="/pool-pump-repair/cedar-park/">Cedar Park pump repair page</a> for how pollen
      season affects equipment) can mean more frequent basket checks even if total run time drops.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Winter.</strong> 4–6 hours is often sufficient in Central Texas, since we rarely
      see extended freezes that would require different run strategies entirely.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>How variable-speed changes the answer</h2>
  <p>
    A variable-speed pump changes the question from "how many hours" to "how many hours at what
    speed." Running longer at a lower speed can achieve the same or better filtration than fewer
    hours at full power, while using less electricity — see our guide on{' '}
    <a href="/guides/variable-speed-vs-single-speed-pool-pump-texas/">variable-speed payback
    math</a> for how that trade-off plays out over a full run season. In practice, many
    variable-speed schedules run 10–12 hours a day at low speed with a shorter high-speed burst
    for tasks like vacuuming or heating.
  </p>

  <h2>Signs your current run time is too short</h2>
  <p>
    Chlorine that drops faster than expected between tests, filter pressure that climbs slower
    than it should (a sign water isn't moving through it enough), or cloudiness that returns
    despite otherwise normal chemistry (see our{' '}
    <a href="/guides/cloudy-pool-water-causes/">cloudy pool water guide</a>) are all signs worth
    checking your run schedule against, not just your chemical routine.
  </p>

  <h2>When to call a pro</h2>
  <p>
    Adjusting your pump timer is safe to experiment with yourself. Call in a technician if you've
    increased run time and the pool still isn't holding chemistry — that usually points to an
    equipment or chemistry issue beyond scheduling, like the CYA buildup covered in our{' '}
    <a href="/guides/pool-shock-not-working-cya/">shock treatment guide</a>, rather than something
    more run time alone will fix. {SITE.feeLine}
  </p>

  <section class="faq">
    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    <h3>Is it more expensive to run the pump longer each day?</h3>
    <p>
      On a single-speed pump, yes, roughly proportional to the hours added. On a variable-speed
      pump, running longer at a lower speed can actually cost less than fewer hours at full
      power — see our variable-speed guide for the underlying math.
    </p>
    <h3>Can I split run time into two shorter sessions instead of one long one?</h3>
    <p>
      Yes, and some pools benefit from a split schedule — for example, a shorter midday run plus
      a longer overnight run — as long as the total daily runtime still meets your pool's needs
      for the season.
    </p>
    <h3>Does a bigger pump mean I can run it for less time?</h3>
    <p>
      Not necessarily — turnover time depends on the pump's actual flow rate relative to your
      pool's volume, not horsepower alone. A correctly sized pump for your pool is what
      determines the real number, which is part of what we check during a diagnosis visit.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Dispatch problem="Not sure the pump is running long enough" area={SITE.serviceArea} />
</Base>
